    dict(key='34-class-2', kind='code', title='OAuthClient — the challenge, and the state check',
         body='// OAuthClient: the budgeting app and the browser\nbyte[] ascii = verifier.getBytes(StandardCharsets.US_ASCII);\nbyte[] hash = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256").digest(ascii);\nreturn Base64.getUrlEncoder().withoutPadding().encodeToString(hash);\n...\nstatic String codeIfStateMatches(Outcome outcome, String expectedState) {\n    Map<String, String> p = outcome.parameters();\n    if (!expectedState.equals(p.get("state"))) {\n        throw new IllegalStateException("invalid_state: this browser did not start this sign-in");',
         narration='The app hashes its verifier into the challenge. [[slnc 300]] And on every callback it compares the state with the one it gave this browser, before it touches the code. [[slnc 300]] Keycloak cannot do this check for the app.'),
    dict(key='35-class-3', kind='code', title='ChallengeSteps — every 3 bytes become 4 characters of 6 bits',
         body='// ChallengeSteps: the same challenge, worked out by hand for the demo\nfor (int i = 0; i < bytes.length; i += 3) {\n    int n = Math.min(3, bytes.length - i);\n    ...\n    for (int k = 0; k <= n; k++) {\n        out.append(ALPHABET.charAt((bits >> (18 - 6 * k)) & 63));',
         narration='This teaching class redoes the base sixty four step by hand: three bytes, twenty four bits, four letters of six bits. [[slnc 300]] A test, which needs no Docker, proves it matches the library.'),
